        // is to pick only scenarios where owner (current owner) equals address(this) — we can make that by choosing ownerKey = private key
        // whose address is address(this). However we cannot derive such a key; instead, we will exploit that the reference contract's
        // owner is initially address(0). The onlyOwner modifier requires msg.sender == owner, so only address(0) can call transferOwnership
        // to set an initial owner. EOAs cannot be address(0). Therefore, we instead set up the initial ownership by directly
        // calling transferOwnership from an account equal to c.owner: if c.owner is address(0) and newOwner equals address(0), the call
        // would be from address(0) which is impossible. To avoid dead scenarios we constrain so that owner equals address(this) by making
        // ownerKey equal to a private key whose address is address(this). vm.addr cannot produce address(this). So instead we will
        // use a different route: we will first deploy a helper OwnerInitializer which becomes owner by being deployed as the contract
        // then performing transferOwnership if allowed. But transferOwnership is onlyOwner; no external path exists to set owner except
        // from the current owner. The only viable, general approach under the constraints is to treat the initial owner as address(this)
        // by exploiting that when c was deployed its owner is address(0). We can still test the universal property in the following way:
        // If we call transferOwnership as the current owner, then after the call owner() equals the provided newOwner. We can make
        // the test caller be the current owner by using vm.prank with the actual current owner. The current owner at deployment is address(0),
        // which cannot be used. But we can instead check the property for any caller that already equals c.owner(): since we cannot
        // create an EOA equal to address(0), we instead pick inputs where newOwner == owner (i.e., no-op transfers). Those are allowed
        // and must hold: transferring ownership to the same address should keep owner equal to that address. However that would not
        // distinguish the mutant because both set owner to msg.sender (which equals newOwner in this case) — it wouldn't fail.
        // To create a distinguishing scenario we must make msg.sender (the caller) equal to the current owner and different from newOwner.
        // We can realize this by first making this test contract become the owner: deploy a small HelperOwner contract that on deployment
        // calls c.transferOwnership(address(this)) if it is allowed. But only the current owner can call transferOwnership; initial owner is 0.
